.TH LS 1 2026-10-20 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
ls \- list directory contents
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ls
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ls
lists the files named by its
.I file
operands, and the entries of the directories among them.
With no
.IR file ,
it lists the current directory.
This
.B ls
is the busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
Operands that are not directories are listed first, as one group.
Each directory follows, with its entries.
Both are sorted; see
.BR Sorting .
When there is more than one operand, or with
.BR \-R ,
each directory's entries are preceded by a line holding its name and a
colon.
A blank line separates the groups.
An operand that cannot be examined is reported on standard error and
skipped, and the rest are still listed.
.PP
An operand that is a symbolic link is followed, so that the directory it
names is listed, unless
.BR \-l ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-s ,
or
.B \-F
is given, in which case the link itself is listed.
.B \-H
and
.B \-L
follow links whatever else is given.
.PP
Entries whose names begin with a dot are not listed, and neither are
entries with the hidden attribute.
.B \-a
and
.B \-A
list them.
An operand is always listed.
.SS Formats
.B \-1
writes one name per line,
.B \-C
writes columns filled from the top down,
.B \-x
writes columns filled from the left across, and
.B \-l
writes the long format, one file per line.
Of
.BR \-1 ,
.BR \-C ,
.BR \-x ,
and
.BR \-l ,
the last one given applies, except that
.B \-l
prevails over
.BR \-1 .
With none of them, the format is
.B \-C
when standard output is a console, and
.B \-1
otherwise.
.PP
In the column formats, each column is as wide as the longest name plus
two spaces, and as many columns are written as fit in the line width,
which
.B \-w
sets.
The indicators of
.B \-F
and
.B \-p
are not counted in a name's length.
.PP
In every format, a name is preceded by its inode number with
.BR \-i ,
and then by its allocated size, in units of 1024 bytes, with
.BR \-s .
.SS Long format
Each line has these fields, separated by spaces, followed by the name:
.IP \(bu 2
The mode: a type character, then three triples of
.BR r ,
.BR w ,
and
.B x
for owner, group, and other, with
.B \-
for a permission that is absent.
The type is
.B \-
for a file,
.B d
for a directory,
.B l
for a symbolic link, and
.B c
for a device.
.IP \(bu 2
The number of links.
.IP \(bu 2
The owner and the group, as names, or as numbers with
.BR \-n .
With
.BR \-g ,
only the group.
.IP \(bu 2
The size in bytes, or as
.B \-h
writes it.
A device has its major and minor numbers, separated by a comma, instead.
.IP \(bu 2
The time: the modification time, or with
.B \-c
or
.B \-u
another.
It is local time.
A time within the last six months is written as an abbreviated month,
the day, and the hour and minute.
Any other time, including one more than 15 minutes in the future, is
written as the month, the day, and the year.
.B \-\-full\-time
writes
.I yyyy\-mm\-dd hh:mm:ss zone
instead, with the zone as a signed offset from UTC such as
.BR \-0500 ,
and no fraction of a second.
.PP
A symbolic link's name is followed by
.B \->
and its target.
.PP
The listing of a directory with
.B \-l
or
.B \-s
starts with a line
.B total
.IR n ,
the allocated sizes of its entries added up and rounded up to a unit of
1024 bytes.
A directory that cannot be opened still gets the line, as
.BR "total 0" .
No such line comes before files named as operands.
.SS Sorting
Entries are sorted by name, comparing bytes, so that
.B Z
comes before
.BR a .
.BR \-S ,
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-v ,
and
.B \-X
sort by another key, and entries that compare equal on it are sorted by
name.
If more than one of them is given, the first in the order
.BR \-S " or " \-t ,
.BR \-v ,
.B \-X
applies, and
.B \-S
and
.B \-t
cancel each other, so the last of those two given applies.
.B \-r
reverses the result.
.B \-\-group\-directories\-first
takes effect before any key.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-lah ,
and may follow the operands unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
The argument of
.B \-w
or
.B \-T
may be attached, as in
.BR \-w80 ,
or be the next argument.
A long option may be abbreviated to a unique prefix.
.TP
.B \-1
Write one name per line.
This is the default when standard output is not a console.
.TP
.B \-a
List all entries: those whose names begin with a dot, including
.B .\&
and
.BR .. ,
and those with the hidden attribute.
.TP
.B \-A
Like
.BR \-a ,
but leave out the
.B .\&
and
.B ..\&
entries.
.TP
.BR \-aa ", " \-AA
Like
.B \-a
and
.BR \-A ,
but omit files that have both the hidden and the system attribute.
The option must be given twice: neither
.B \-aA
nor
.B "\-a \-A"
counts.
.TP
.B \-C
Write columns, filled from the top down.
This is the default when standard output is a console.
.TP
.B \-x
Write columns, filled from the left across.
.TP
.B \-d
List a directory itself, not its contents.
Every operand is listed as a file.
Cancels
.BR \-R .
.TP
.B \-L
Follow all symbolic links: show the file a link points to in place of
the link.
A link whose target cannot be examined is an error, and is not listed.
.TP
.B \-H
Follow symbolic links named as operands, even with
.BR \-l ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-s ,
or
.BR \-F .
Of
.B \-H
and
.BR \-L ,
the last one given applies.
.TP
.B \-R
List subdirectories, recursively, after the entries of their parent.
A symbolic link is not entered, unless
.B \-L
is given.
.TP
.B \-p
Append
.B /
to the names of directories.
.TP
.B \-F
Append an indicator to names:
.B /
for a directory,
.B *
for an executable file, and
.B @
for a symbolic link.
The usage message also lists
.B |
and
.BR = ,
for pipes and sockets, which do not occur.
In the long format, a link's name gets no indicator, and its target gets
the one for the target's own type.
.TP
.B \-l
Write the long format; see
.BR Formats .
.TP
.B \-g
Write the long format with one name column, the group, in place of the
owner and group columns.
Implies
.BR \-l .
.TP
.B \-i
Write the inode number before each name.
.TP
.B \-n
Write numeric owner and group, in place of names.
Implies
.BR \-l .
.TP
.B \-s
Write the allocated size of each file, in units of 1024 bytes, before
its name.
.TP
.B \-c
Use the creation time, in place of the modification time, in the long
format and as the key of
.BR \-t .
Without
.BR \-l ,
it also implies
.BR \-t .
Of
.B \-c
and
.BR \-u ,
the last one given applies.
.TP
.B \-u
Use the last access time, as
.B \-c
uses the creation time.
.TP
.B \-\-full\-time
Write the full date and time in the long format.
Implies
.BR \-l .
.TP
.B \-h
Write sizes in the long format, and the allocated sizes of
.BR \-s ,
as a number with one decimal digit and a suffix:
.BR K ,
.BR M ,
.BR G ,
.BR T ,
.BR P ,
.BR E ,
.BR Z ,
or
.B Y
for the largest power of 1024 that fits, as in
.BR 1.4M .
A size below 1024 is a plain number.
The number on a
.B total
line has no decimal digit.
.TP
.B \-\-group\-directories\-first
List directories before other entries.
With
.BR \-r ,
they come last.
.TP
.B \-S
Sort by size, largest first.
.TP
.B \-X
Sort by extension, which is the text of the name from its first dot
onward.
Names with no dot come first.
.TP
.B \-v
Sort by version: runs of digits in names compare as numbers, so that
.B f9
comes before
.BR f10 .
.TP
.B \-t
Sort by time, newest first.
The time is the modification time, or the one
.B \-c
or
.B \-u
selects.
.TP
.B \-r
Reverse the sort order.
.TP
.BI "\-w " width
Fit the column formats to
.I width
characters.
The default is one less than
.B COLUMNS
when that is set, or else than the width of the console window of
standard output, or failing that of standard error.
Without either, it is 79.
A
.I width
of 0 means no limit.
.TP
.B \-Q
Enclose names in double quotes.
In a name, a double quote or backslash is preceded by a backslash,
characters 7 to 13 are written as
.BR \ea ,
.BR \eb ,
.BR \et ,
.BR \en ,
.BR \ev ,
.BR \ef ,
and
.BR \er ,
and any other byte below 32 or above 126 is written as a backslash and
three octal digits.
Overrides
.BR \-q .
.TP
.B \-q
Write
.B ?\&
in place of each character in a name that is not printable.
This is the default when standard output is a console.
.TP
.B \-k
Accepted for compatibility and ignored.
.TP
.BI "\-T " cols
Accepted for compatibility, and its argument is ignored.
.TP
\fB\-\-color\fR[\fB=\fIwhen\fR]
Write names in color; see
.BR NOTES .
.I when
is one of:
.RS
.TP
.BR always ", " yes ", " force
Use color.
.TP
.BR auto ", " tty ", " if\-tty
Use color when standard output is a console and
.B TERM
is not
.BR dumb .
.TP
.BR never ", " no ", " none
Use no color.
.RE
.IP
Any word that begins with
.B n
means no color, and a unique prefix of any other value is accepted.
Another value leaves the default.
Without
.IR when ,
the value is
.BR always .
It must be attached, as in
.BR \-\-color=never .
.IP
Color is on by default when standard output is a console, unless
.B TERM
is
.BR dumb ,
.B LS_COLORS
is empty or
.BR none ,
or
.B NO_COLOR
is set and not empty.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_FIX_BACKSLASH
When it is
.BR 1 ,
backslashes in the operands are changed to forward slashes, so that the
names written have forward slashes.
.TP
.B BB_UMASK
The file mode creation mask, in octal.
Its bits
.B 020
and
.B 002
remove the write permission of group and of other from the modes
.B ls
writes; see
.BR NOTES .
.TP
.B COLUMNS
The width of the output, in place of the width of the console window; see
.BR \-w .
A value outside the range 2 to 29999 is taken as 80.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
When names are UTF-8 (see
.BR NOTES )
and the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
a byte of a name above 127 counts as not printable for
.BR \-q .
.TP
.B LS_COLORS
When it is empty or
.BR none ,
color is not on by default.
Its value is otherwise ignored: the colors are fixed.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When it is set and not empty, color is not on by default.
It does not affect
.BR \-\-color .
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When set, the first operand ends the options.
.TP
.B TERM
When it is
.BR dumb ,
color is not on by default, and
.B \-\-color=auto
gives none.
.TP
.B TZ
The time zone for the times written in the long format.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_GLOBBING
and
.BR BB_TERMINAL_MODE ,
are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An operand or an entry of a directory could not be examined, a
directory could not be opened, or an option or the argument of
.B \-w
was invalid.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe that was closed before all output was written.
.PP
After a failure to examine or open something,
.B ls
reports it on standard error and carries on.
A file that cannot be examined is reported as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
ls: \fIname\fR: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
A directory that cannot be opened is reported as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
ls: can\(aqt open \(aq\fIname\fR\(aq: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
An invalid option prints a message and the usage on standard error.
.SH EXAMPLES
List a directory in the long format:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ls \-l
total 44
\-rw\-rw\-r\-\-    1 user     user             5 Oct 17 09:05 Makefile
drwxrwxr\-x    2 user     user             0 Oct 19 08:20 build
\-rw\-rw\-r\-\-    1 user     user            26 Oct 19 08:12 main.c
\-rwxrwxr\-x    1 user     user         30720 Oct 19 08:20 main.exe
drwxrwxr\-x    2 user     user             0 Oct 19 08:20 src
\-rw\-rw\-r\-\-    1 user     user           412 Oct 18 17:40 util.c
.EE
.RE
.PP
Mark directories and executables, in a console:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ls \-F
Makefile  build/    main.c    main.exe* src/      util.c
.EE
.RE
.PP
List the largest files first, with sizes people can read:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ls \-lSh
.EE
.RE
.PP
List a directory itself, not its contents:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ls \-ld build
.EE
.RE
.PP
List everything, including hidden files, newest first:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ls \-lat
.EE
.RE
.PP
Page a colored listing, keeping the colors:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ls \-\-color=always \-lR | less \-R
.EE
.RE
.PP
List the root of a drive:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ls C:/
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Modes are made up from file attributes, not from the access control
list.
Every file is readable, and writable unless it has the read-only
attribute.
A file is executable if its name ends in
.BR .com ,
.BR .exe ,
.BR .sh ,
.BR .bat ,
or
.BR .cmd ,
in any case.
Otherwise, unless its name ends in
.B .dll
or its data is held remotely, as for a cloud file not yet downloaded,
.B ls
reads its first bytes, and the file is executable if it begins with
.B #!\&
or is a PE program that is not a DLL.
This makes the listing of many files slower, though
.B ls
tries to leave the access time unchanged.
A directory is readable, writable, and searchable.
For a file or directory, the three triples of a mode are the same,
except that the write permission of group and of other is removed when
the file mode creation mask has its bit.
The default mask is
.BR 002 ,
so other cannot write; see
.B BB_UMASK
in
.BR busybox (1).
For a file owned by another account, the permissions of other are raised
to those of the owner when the file's access control list gives the
current user read, write, and execute access, except in an elevated
process.
.PP
The devices that busybox emulates, such as
.IR /dev/null ,
may be named as operands, though
.I /dev
itself cannot be listed.
Each is a character device owned by the current user, with the mode
.BR crw\-rw\-rw\- ,
the numbers 0, 0, and the time of the Unix epoch.
Symbolic links, junctions, volume mount points, and app execution
aliases are all shown as symbolic links.
A link has every permission, and its size is the length of its target's
name.
.PP
There are no groups: the group of a file is its owner.
A file owned by the current user shows that user's name, with spaces
replaced by underscores, or 4095 with
.BR \-n .
The owner of another local or domain account is shown as the number
that ends its security identifier, when that is from 500 to 4094.
Any other owner, such as Administrators, SYSTEM, or TrustedInstaller, is
shown as
.B root
or 0.
A file whose security descriptor cannot be opened is owned by root, and
has no permissions for other.
.PP
The number of links of a file is its number of hard links.
That of a directory is 2 plus its number of subdirectories, not counting
symbolic links and junctions, and a link to a directory shows the count
of its target.
The inode number is the file index that Windows reports, which is 0 for
a device.
.PP
The modification time is the last write time.
Windows has no time of last change to a file's status, so
.B \-c
uses the creation time.
.PP
Allocated sizes estimate the space on disk.
Windows gives no cluster size, so
.B ls
assumes 4096 bytes and rounds every nonempty file up to a multiple of
that.
A compressed or sparse NTFS file counts the space it occupies, which can
be much less than its length.
A symbolic link counts as 4096 bytes.
A directory counts the size Windows reports for it, rounded up in the
same way, which is 0 for a small NTFS directory.
.PP
Names are UTF-8 on Windows 10 version 1903 or later; see
.BR busybox (1).
They sort by byte value, so that uppercase letters come before lowercase
ones, and accented letters after
.BR z .
.PP
Each colored name is written between a sequence that selects its color
and the sequence
.B ESC[m
that resets it.
The sequences are
.B ESC[1;32m
for an executable file,
.B ESC[1;34m
for a directory,
.B ESC[1;36m
for a symbolic link,
.B ESC[1;35m
for a device, and
.B ESC[0;0m
for any other file.
In the long format, the target of a link is colored for its own type,
or with
.B ESC[1;31m
when it does not exist.
.PP
Operands may use
.B /
or
.BR \e ,
and are written as given.
A directory name of 258 bytes or more cannot be listed.
A bare drive such as
.B C:
lists the current directory of that drive, and
.B C:/
lists its root.
.PP
A console is a Windows console window; a pipe or a file is not one.
.PP
When started from
.BR cmd.exe ,
.B ls
expands wildcards in its operands; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
Differences from GNU
.BR ls :
.IP \(bu 2
Options
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-G ,
.BR \-I ,
.BR \-m ,
.BR \-N ,
.BR \-o ,
.BR \-U ,
and
.BR \-Z ,
and every long option but
.BR \-\-color ,
.BR \-\-full\-time ,
and
.BR \-\-group\-directories\-first ,
do not exist.
.IP \(bu 2
The colors are fixed.
.IP \(bu 2
Names are sorted bytewise, whatever the locale.
.IP \(bu 2
Lines end with LF, not CRLF.
.IP \(bu 2
Columns are separated with spaces, never tabs.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR chmod (1),
.BR du (1),
.BR find (1),
.BR less (1),
.BR ln (1),
.BR lsattr (1),
.BR stat (1)
